<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dinh Phe De</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/974">974</a>–<a href="/years/1001">1001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Dinh Toan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dinh Phe De, born Dinh Toan, was the second and last emperor of the Dinh dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, put on the throne as a child after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Dinh Tien Hoang</a> was murdered in 979. Facing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> invasion, the court and his mother made the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Le Hoan</a> emperor in 980, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-le-dynasty">Early Le dynasty</a>. He was killed in 1001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinh-phe-de.jpg" alt="Dinh Phe De" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kien1980v at Vietnamese Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinhtoan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Đinh Toàn, also recorded as Đinh Tuệ, was born in 974 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lư</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Đinh Bộ Lĩnh</a> and of the empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duong-van-nga">Dương Vân Nga</a>, one of five consorts on whom Đinh Bộ Lĩnh conferred that title. He was the second son, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-lien">Đinh Liễn</a> and before Đinh Hạng Lang. When Đinh Bộ Lĩnh named Đinh Hạng Lang crown prince, Đinh Liễn killed his youngest brother in anger. A minor official, Đỗ Thích, then dreamed that he would take the throne and assassinated both Đinh Bộ Lĩnh and Đinh Liễn, leaving Đinh Toàn the last heir, enthroned at the age of six.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As a child he was emperor in name only, and all power lay with the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Hoàn</a>. Some modern historians suggest that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duong-van-nga">Dương Vân Nga</a> plotted with Lê Hoàn in the killings to put her son on the throne, but this is not confirmed. Two officials, Đinh Điền and Nguyễn Bặc, feared that Lê Hoàn would replace the dynasty and suspected him of a liaison with the dowager empress; they moved against him and were quickly crushed.</p><p>The prince consort Ngô Nhật Khánh fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> and persuaded its monarch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameshvaravarman-i">Parameshvaravarman I</a>, to send a fleet against the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-lu">Hoa Lư</a>, but a sudden storm sank many of the ships. In 980, hearing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Đinh Bộ Lĩnh</a>'s death, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperor sent an army to invade Đại Cồ Việt, as the country was then called. Because the emperor was too young, the dowager empress and the court enthroned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-dai-hanh">Lê Hoàn</a>, who as Lê Đại Hành fought the Song army twice and drove the invaders out, and with his accession the Đinh dynasty came to an end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After he was deposed, Đinh Toàn received the noble title Vệ Vương. In 1001, while putting down the rebellion of Cửu Long in Thanh Hóa Province, he was struck by an arrow and died at once, at the age of 27. Later historical works gave him the name by which he is known, Đinh Phế Đế, the deposed emperor of the Đinh dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Đinh Phế Đế” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 979 Dinh Tien Hoang and his eldest son were assassinated, leaving the child <b>Dinh Phe De</b> on the throne.</blockquote><a href="/state/dinh-dynasty">Dinh dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hoa Lư Ancient Capital, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Hoa Lư Ancient Capital, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.26,105.95&amp;year=991" aria-label="Hoa Lư Ancient Capital on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Dinh Phe De"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3884 5761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3884 5761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.26,105.95&amp;year=991">What was here in 991 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
